    </edge>
    <edge id="e11" source="ont:ordini" target="ont:external-forces">
      <data key="ek0">claim:d1c7-external-forces</data>
      <data key="ek1">machiavelli</data>
      <data key="ek2">Whenever a party within a city calls in external forces, the cause is defective civic orders: no internal channel exists to vent malignant humors ordinarily.</data>
      <data key="ek3">negative</data>
      <data key="ek4">empirical-generalization</data>
      <data key="ek5">disc:it:1:7</data>
      <data key="ek6">historically situated claim, not established causal fact</data>
    </edge>
    <edge id="e12" source="ont:accusation-channels" target="ont:external-forces">
      <data key="ek0">claim:d1c7-external-forces</data>
      <data key="ek1">machiavelli</data>
      <data key="ek2">Whenever a party within a city calls in external forces, the cause is defective civic orders: no internal channel exists to vent malignant humors ordinarily.</data>
      <data key="ek3">negative</data>
      <data key="ek4">empirical-generalization</data>
      <data key="ek5">disc:it:1:7</data>
      <data key="ek6">historically situated claim, not established causal fact</data>
    </edge>
    <edge id="e13" source="ont:accusation-channels" target="ont:institutional-survival">
      <data key="ek0">claim:d1c7-few-judges</data>
      <data key="ek1">machiavelli</data>
      <data key="ek2">Accusation before few judges is ineffective because the few act in the interest of the few; accusations must lie before many judges to be credible.</data>
      <data key="ek3">conditional</data>
      <data key="ek4">causal-mechanism</data>
      <data key="ek5">disc:it:1:7</data>
      <data key="ek6">historically situated claim, not established causal fact</data>
    </edge>
    <edge id="e14" source="ont:grain-distribution-control" target="ont:tribunate">
      <data key="ek0">claim:l2c34-livy-counterfactual</data>
      <data key="ek1">livy</data>
      <data key="ek2">Livy's own counterfactual: by conceding a lower grain price, the senators could probably have rid themselves of the tribunate and all the rights imposed on them against their will; whether that would have been right is harder to say.</data>
      <data key="ek3">positive</data>
      <data key="ek4">counterfactual</data>
      <data key="ek5">livy:la:2:34</data>
      <data key="ek6">historically situated claim, not established causal fact</data>
    </edge>
    <edge id="e15" source="ont:reputation-private" target="ont:partisans">
      <data key="ek0">claim:d3c28-private-benefits-tyranny</data>
      <data key="ek1">machiavelli</data>
      <data key="ek2">Works that appear pious (like feeding the hungry) can conceal the beginning of tyranny: private benefits create partisans and embolden the benefactor to corrupt the public and force the laws.</data>
      <data key="ek3">positive</data>
      <data key="ek4">causal-mechanism</data>
      <data key="ek5">disc:it:3:28</data>
      <data key="ek6">historically situated claim, not established causal fact</data>
    </edge>
